spans.s:
Varuṇa-gṛhīta, ‘seized by Varuṇa,’ is found in several
passages^1 as a description of a man afflicted with dropsy, which
is the disease sent by Varuṇa as a punishment for sin.^2
1) Taittirīya Saṃhitā, ii. 1, 2, 1;
vi. 4, 2, 3; Kāṭhaka Saṃhitā, xii. 4;
Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa, iv. 4, 5, 11;
Taittirīya Brāhmaṇa, i. 6, 4, 1, etc.
2) Rv. vi. 74, 4; vii. 88. 7; Av. ii. 10, 1;
iv. 16, 6. 7; xiv. 1, 57; 2, 49, etc.
Cf. Oldenberg, Religion des Veda, 203;
Macdonell, Vedic Mythology, p. 29, n. 16.

Varṣa denotes primarily ‘rain,’^1 then ‘rainy season’^2 and
‘year.’^3
1) Neuter: Rv. v. 58, 7; 83, 10;
Av. iii. 27, 6; iv. 15, 2, etc.
2) Feminine plural; Av. vi. 55, 2;
Taittirīya Saṃhitā, i. 6, 2, 3; ii. 6, 1, 1;
v. 6, 10, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, x. 12,
etc.
3) Aitareya Brāhmaṇa, iv. 17, 5;
Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa, i. 9, 3, 19, etc.
